Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:
Two Registered Tests of an Audit Rule
Ömer Demirel
Draft 9, November 2026

Abstract
A buyer of computation often cannot see the quality of delivered work and can afford to
check only a sample of it. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly
exact audits, and free identity replacement, in a mechanism class without entry fees, identity
certification, or collateral before receipts; a failed audit refunds the job’s escrow and bans the
serving identity for a finite number of rounds. For the uniform-audit baselines with refundand-ban enforcement and no bonds, immediate free replacement makes the ban costless against
honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, when re-entry comes before the
next allocation. The rule we study, R3, checks every job of a new identity while the round’s
audits allow it, and after that window keeps targeting the newest identities, so a replacement
can stay under heavy inspection: persistent age-ranked targeting rather than release by age. We
report two registered tests. In the first, the original comparative claim failed; an amended claim,
registered after development results were seen, passed on the public identity-replacement family.
The second test compares R3 with a verified-history comparator C∗ at matched budget, payment,
tolerance, ban, pacing, and sanctions, decomposes R3 into its three parts, adds stronger attacks,
and repeats the B3–R3 comparison on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin
block hash. Pooled over the six fresh markets, the registered procedure classifies R3 and C∗ as
“equivalent, direction R3 lower” within the pre-set materiality margin of ±0.05: C∗ minus R3 is
0.021 under D2+H3 and 0.024 under X7-SC, almost all of it in the profit the attacker earns when
it deviates. The registered intervals hold the six markets fixed; treated as a sample of markets,
the per-market differences give a t interval of [−0.013, 0.054] under D2+H3 (exploratory), so
this analysis does not establish the ordering or the equivalence for the neighbourhood. The
X7-SC search returned the D2+H3 winner it starts from in 10 of 12 Q1 cells, so the two families
are close to one test. C∗ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the
runner-up left a lower development gain. R3’s two targeting parts are substitutes (factorial
contrast 0.082): once newest-first targeting is in place, the priority phase changes the gain by
only −0.001; pacing slightly raises the measured gain. At the headline market, the selected
sealed-attacker policy gains 0.069 against R3, the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers
R3’s gross efficiency to 0.840, and similarly for B3 and C∗ , and the registered welfare search
under a profitability constraint found no profitable attack against R3. The B3–R3 comparison
passes on 3 of the six fresh markets, all of them qualified as results of the prescribed search
procedure. All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references and are conditional
on the sampled markets, the stated neighbourhood, the fixed rule parameters, the searched
attacker families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities.

1

Introduction

A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost
and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are
1

expensive and run after the response is served. FlashProve, for example, proves that a committed
integer model produced a given token under greedy decoding; the provider generates proofs after the
response is served, at about 70 to 95 times the cost of one forward pass [27, p. 18], for a window of
1024 tokens per request, and the client checks each proof on a CPU [27, pp. 16–17]. Its Lean proofs
cover the protocol; the equivalence of the executable prover and verifier to it is not proved [27, p. 17].
Such proofs detect a substituted computation; they do not prevent an incorrect response from being
served. If the checking party funds such checks, a budget forces a sample, and the questions become
which jobs to check and how to sanction a provider whose check fails.
We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identities are
unavailable. The mechanism class excludes entry fees, identity certification, and collateral before
receipts, and a sanctioned provider may re-enter at once under a fresh identity. The rules refund the
escrow of a failed job and exclude the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. The population
is a fixed set of providers, each with one active identity at a time; concurrent aliases, pre-aged
reserve identities, and additional entrants (Sybil attacks [10]) are outside the model.
Checking newcomers is an old idea. Intensive verification until an identity has earned credibility is established: credibility-based spot-checking analyses rejoining under new identities [28, 29];
BOINC offers an optional adaptive-replication policy that replicates a host’s jobs until the host
passes a trust threshold of consecutive valid results [2, 1]; customs guidance recommends checking
all declarations of new importers for a period [35]. Checking can also depend on what a participant
reports [40]; our admissible rules may read offers and claims, but we study targeting by identity age
and verified history. The question we ask is narrower: under a hard audit budget, does R3’s persistent age-ranked targeting do better or worse than targeting by verified history, when everything
else is held equal?
The rule. For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, immediate free replacement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to identity relabelling,
so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary 1). R3 gives audit priority to the jobs of identities in their first L rounds, auditing all of them when the round’s
quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established identities. Its
priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement is uniquely
the newest established identity, it receives at least min(s, ⌈r/2⌉) of the r residual audits, s being
its served jobs (Lemma 2 and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a replacement
receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer entrants, or a
quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity’s first offer.
Two registered tests. Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped
before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments.
The first test (October 2026) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results
then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second
test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their
seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition,
stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test’s comparison.
Contributions. Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker
gain within the pre-set materiality margin of ±0.05; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first,
2


