the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C∗ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option to
replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against them.
These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episodelevel mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, has
the probability-weighted mean of its components’ gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain
constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing
evaluation records, and they show that the W1 policy class is not closed under randomisation.
With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation records, the mixtures
reach gross efficiencies of 0.932 for R3 and 0.921 for C∗ at non-negative estimated gain; with public
D2+H3 companions, R3’s mixture (0.945) meets the registered training constraint, and C∗ ’s does
so at the smaller weight 47.65 percent, with efficiency 0.9224 against 0.997 under the registered W1
search (Tables 13 and 14). Weights and companions were chosen on the evaluation seeds with an
unadjusted nominal one-sided 95 percent bound, so these rows show that the W1 policy class is
not closed under randomisation; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability
remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above 0.90; the analysis does not touch Q4 or
its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are
0.584 against B3 and 0.058 against R3 (search flagged); for five, 0.191 and 0.108 (total objective),
while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3’s worst member a mean
gain of −0.001; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives 0.024 against B3 and 0.025 against R3.
These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family
(Table 17). At ρ = 1, where R3’s terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves 0.565 under D2+H3; the
repair R4 lowers this to 0.356 and C∗ with the capped schedule (C∗ /S2) to 0.434 (Table 18).

